  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1247 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nUpper ridging centered over the Red River Valley is gradually\nbuilding into the Mid-South. Weak northwest flow is in place\nacross the Mid-South. Satellite imagery and the RAP indicate an\nMCV  over northern Arkansas this morning. This feature will track\ninto the Mid-South this afternoon and evening and interact with\nan unstable airmass with MLCAPEs over 2000 J/kg. This will result\nin isolated showers and thunderstorms. Hi-res models show sparse\ncoverage, but given the last couple of days and precipitable\nwater values still around 1.7 inches, it seems prudent to add\nsmall pops to the forecast this afternoon. Agitated cu is already\nnoted from the Missouri Bootheel southward into north Mississippi.\n\nThe big story is the near-record/record heat that will prevail\nwell into the upcoming week as the upper ridge continues to build\nover the region.  Temperatures are quickly climbing into the 90s\nthis morning, with dewpoints in the lower to mid 70s. Heat index\nvalues are already topping 100 degrees in several places at noon.\nThere is a Heat Advisory in effect until 7 pm this evening. The\nheat will intensify Monday and Tuesday as highs flirt with triple\ndigits over much of the area. Several record high temperatures\nwill likely be tied or broken over the next few days. An\nadditional Heat Advisory is in effect Monday through Tuesday for\nheat index values above 105 degrees. A weak disturbance rotating\nunder the upper ridge that will be centered over the Mid-South on\nMonday, will kick off isolated showers and thunderstorms across\nparts of north Mississippi and east-central Arkansas Monday\nafternoon. However, they will do little but provide very\nlocalized relief from the heat.\n\nHeat headlines will likely be extended into at least Wednesday.\nToward the end of the week, surface ridging over the Southeast\nU.S. and associated southeasterly flow may introduce some lower\ndewpoints. Temperatures will still be close to record highs\nthrough at least Saturday, but with the humidity knocked down a\nnotch, heat index values will likely not reach 105 degrees.\n\nA disturbance tracking across the Gulf late this week into next\nweekend, combined with a trough moving through the center of the\ncountry, may start to bring some heat relief by next Sunday. The\nupper ridge will likely weaken, and the chance for showers and\nthunderstorms should be on the increase by then, with\ntemperatures trending to more normal levels.",
